# Supabase — Dashboard → Project Settings → Data API / API Keys
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill both values in. The storefront reads the
# catalogue (fragrances + their per-size prices and stock) from Supabase in
# Server Components only, so the names are deliberately *unprefixed*: Next
# inlines only NEXT_PUBLIC_* into the client bundle, which means these cannot
# reach the browser by construction.
#
# Use the anon / publishable key, never the service_role or sb_secret_ key —
# those bypass Row Level Security entirely. The anon key only grants what the
# policies in perfumora-admin/supabase/schema.sql allow: reading active
# fragrances and their size rows.
#
# SUPABASE_URL is the project origin only, with no /rest/v1 suffix — the client
# appends that itself.
#
# Both must also be set wherever `next build` runs: the catalogue query executes
# at build time as well as on the server.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The service-role key — Dashboard → Project Settings → API keys → `service_role`
# (reveal it there; it is the one marked secret).
#
# This one BYPASSES Row Level Security, so it is not the anon key's sibling: it
# is a database superuser in a string. It exists here for exactly one job —
# writing an order, which `anon` has no policy for by design — and is read only
# by src/app/_lib/supabase-admin.ts, on the server, inside the `placeOrder`
# action. Never give it a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix and never import that module from a
# Client Component; either one publishes it to every visitor.
#
# Leave it blank and the storefront still renders and browses — only placing an
# order fails, with a message naming this key. It must also be set wherever
# `next build` runs and wherever the app is deployed.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
